}],"self_ref":"#/texts/44","text":"-50"},{"children":[],"content_layer":"body","label":"text","orig":"Range Profile (dBm)","parent":{"$ref":"#/pictures/1"},"prov":[{"bbox":{"b":173.48709952520744,"coord_origin":"BOTTOMLEFT","l":65.02153424543496,"r":72.2579677780988,"t":244.52346298415034},"charspan":[0,19],"page_no":1}],"self_ref":"#/texts/45","text":"Range Profile (dBm)"},{"children":[],"content_layer":"body","label":"text","orig":"Range Profile","parent":{"$ref":"#/pictures/1"},"prov":[{"bbox":{"b":268.21434958259306,"coord_origin":"BOTTOMLEFT","l":120.77223489618967,"r":159.54882788952727,"t":274.14972038166366},"charspan":[0,13],"page_no":1}],"self_ref":"#/texts/46","text":"Range Profile"},{"children":[],"content_layer":"body","label":"text","orig":"Expected noise power","parent":{"$ref":"#/pictures/1"},"prov":[{"bbox":{"b":259.42479369137345,"coord_origin":"BOTTOMLEFT","l":120.77223489618967,"r":183.74419294644645,"t":265.36016449044405},"charspan":[0,20],"page_no":1}],"self_ref":"#/texts/47","text":"Expected noise power"},{"children":[],"content_layer":"body","label":"text","orig":"Expected signal Power","parent":{"$ref":"#/pictures/1"},"prov":[{"bbox":{"b":250.63523780104288,"coord_origin":"BOTTOMLEFT","l":120.77223489618967,"r":185.87568939193693,"t":256.5706086001135},"charspan":[0,21],"page_no":1}],"self_ref":"#/texts/48","text":"Expected signal Power"},{"children":[],"content_layer":"body","label":"text","orig":"When multiple targets coexist, as shown in Fig. 11(a), the resultant ISI and ICI from targets beyond the ISI-free range adds up, which may lift the noise level up. However, according to (39), the total ISI/ICI resultant by all the 6 targets beyond the ISI-free distance is only -98 dBm, which slightly lifts the noise level from -87 . 17 dBm to -86 . 83 dBm. In general, when the number of subcarriers is sufficiently large, the impact of ISI and ICI caused by CP limitation is negligible for sensing. According to the specifications in 5G NR, the bandwidth allocated for each user ranges from 34.56MHz to 396MHz when the subcarrier spacing is 120kHz, which corresponds to 288 to 3300 subcarriers. If the number of subcarriers in the simulation reduces from 2048 to 288 in Fig. 11(b), the interference power will increase to -97 . 86 dBm, which is still negligible as compared with the noise level.","parent":{"$ref":"#/body"},"prov":[{"bbox":{"b":44.0507743929204,"coord_origin":"BOTTOMLEFT","l":48.964,"r":300.0219200000001,"t":88.46865260000004},"charspan":[0,247],"page_no":1},{"bbox":{"b":605.94377439292,"coord_origin":"BOTTOMLEFT","l":311.978,"r":563.0409770000002,"t":734.3664558},"charspan":[248,898],"page_no":1}],"self_ref":"#/texts/49","text":"When multiple targets coexist, as shown in Fig. 11(a), the resultant ISI and ICI from targets beyond the ISI-free range adds up, which may lift the noise level up. However, according to (39), the total ISI/ICI resultant by all the 6 targets beyond the ISI-free distance is only -98 dBm, which slightly lifts the noise level from -87 . 17 dBm to -86 . 83 dBm. In general, when the number of subcarriers is sufficiently large, the impact of ISI and ICI caused by CP limitation is negligible for sensing. According to the specifications in 5G NR, the bandwidth allocated for each user ranges from 34.56MHz to 396MHz when the subcarrier spacing is 120kHz, which corresponds to 288 to 3300 subcarriers. If the number of subcarriers in the simulation reduces from 2048 to 288 in Fig. 11(b), the interference power will increase to -97 . 86 dBm, which is still negligible as compared with the noise level."},{"children":[],"content_layer":"body","label":"caption","orig":"Fig. 11: The sensing of multiple targets.","parent":{"$ref":"#/pictures/2"},"prov":[{"bbox":{"b":209.79738450973446,"coord_origin":"BOTTOMLEFT","l":363.167,"r":511.8478447999998,"t":217.49412640000003},"charspan":[0,41],"page_no":1}],"self_ref":"#/texts/50","text":"Fig. 11: The sensing of multiple targets."},{"children":[],"conte